WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.030
Let’s talk about tracking your progress, and planning for flares.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.970
Follow-up visits look at more than one sign of disease activity.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.360
The team may check sore or swollen joints, skin, and tendon sites.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:21.830
They also ask about daily tasks, and your own view of your pain and health.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:28.490
Minimal disease activity, or MDA, uses several measures together.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:32.460
It helps track care. It is not a stage of disease.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:37.560
A quiet skin patch alone does not show the whole picture.

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:45.950
Flares are often part of living with psoriatic disease, but they should not happen often when treatment is working.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:50.980
Keeping a record of symptoms and triggers can help you see flares coming.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:01:00.260
A useful record tracks more than pain: also energy, mood, sleep, and how easy daily tasks feel.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:05.050
Write down what changed, when it began, and how it affects your day.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.170
Make a flare plan with your care team.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:15.590
Ask which changes need a call, which contact route to use, and what to do after hours.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.970
It pays to plan ahead for what could go wrong.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.580
Repeated flares may mean the plan needs a review.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:29.190
Follow the medicine instructions agreed with your prescribing team.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:35.420
Share patterns at follow-up visits. They can show changes that one visit might miss.

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:39.490
Great job! Let’s review.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.230
Follow-up visits look at more than one sign of disease activity.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:49.570
If symptoms return or worsen, discuss a flare plan with the care team.
